{"title":"Distance","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Distance</b> indicates how far apart two objects are. In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Coordinate_geometry\" title=\"Coordinate geometry\">coordinate geometry</a>, the distance between two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Point\" title=\"Point\">points</a> can be determined by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Distance_formula\" title=\"Distance formula\">distance formula</a>.\n</p><p><b>IMPORTANT:</b> Do not confuse distance, a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Scalar\" title=\"Scalar\">scalar</a> value, with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Displacement\" title=\"Displacement\">displacement</a>. Distance, being a scalar, has no direction attributed to it. On the other hand, displacement does. Displacement is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Vector\" title=\"Vector\">vector</a>.\n</p><p>The change of distance over time is said to be its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Speed\" title=\"Speed\">speed</a> (or rate). That is, d/t=r or d=rt.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Distance&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018082843\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.007 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.012 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 7/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 265/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 0/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.336      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.336      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4617:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp